    "slot_key": "Dependency outage and approval closure handling",
    "slot_value": "If a dependent system is unavailable or the approving office is closed, the request should stay pending with a clear reason and timestamp rather than being dropped; urgent matters should use a fallback manual process so the officer can continue and complete the formal step later, while non-urgent items can wait in a queue and be routed automatically once service resumes. Pending items should remain visible in a suspended or pending state so staff can see they are not lost, with the reason for the delay recorded. If the dependency is unavailable only briefly, items can wait in queue; if the outage exceeds an operational threshold, a manual workaround is allowed only for urgent cases and with supervisor awareness. Once the other system or office is back, the items should resume from where they stopped, and the system should track what was completed manually so nothing gets duplicated. Exceptional overrides after transfer, court send, or closure are allowed only for clerical error, new evidence, court return, or supervisory review showing the case was closed too early; ordinary users cannot do this, approval must come from a supervisor or higher-ranking officer, and in some cases both the originating and receiving unit must confirm. All overrides must be logged with reason, approver, and timestamp for audit trail.",
