    "slot_value": "If a dependent system is unavailable or the approving office is closed, the request should stay pending with a clear reason and timestamp rather than being dropped; urgent matters should use a fallback manual process so the officer can continue and complete the formal step later, while non-urgent items can wait in a queue and be routed automatically once service resumes. Pending items should remain visible in a suspended or pending state so staff can see they are not lost, with the reason for the delay recorded. If the dependency is unavailable only briefly, items can wait in queue; if the outage exceeds an operational threshold, a manual workaround is allowed only for urgent cases and with supervisor awareness. Once the other system or office is back, the items should resume from where they stopped, and the system should track what was completed manually so nothing gets duplicated. Exceptional overrides after transfer, court send, or closure are allowed only for clerical error, new evidence, court return, or supervisory review showing the case was closed too early; ordinary users cannot do this, approval must come from a supervisor or higher-ranking officer, and in some cases both the originating and receiving unit must confirm. All overrides must be logged with reason, approver, timestamp, approver identity and role, exact date and time of approval, the user who received the exception, the station or location, the case or incident reference, the specific offline action allowed, the reason for the exception, the expiry or review time if temporary, and whether the approval was made locally or escalated from a higher office for audit trail.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-8-2-dyn-12",
    "slot_key": "Internal SOP compliance for sensitive records management",
    "slot_value": "Must follow internal police and ministry SOPs for access control, audit logging, and case file preservation, with exception handling allowed for urgent operational situations. Only authorized supervisors or designated officers may approve access to redacted or sensitive records. Temporary access renewal should be approved when the investigation is still active, the officer still has a legitimate need to know, and the original access period is about to expire before the work is complete; it also covers court preparation, follow-up witness checks, and pending forensic review where access is still necessary. Renewal authorization would be by the case supervisor or unit head depending on sensitivity, but the exact approval ladder should be confirmed with the ministry. Any bypass of normal controls should trigger escalation and a mandatory after-the-fact review. If audit logging cannot be fully captured in the moment, a manual incident note and late entry of the reason, approver, and affected records must be completed as soon as possible. In the first release, only a small set of trusted administrators should be able to create, edit, disable, or assign roles to users, and those actions should be logged. Changes in access when someone moves to another station or needs access to a different case should require approval by the local supervisor or records lead rather than happening automatically. Cross-station access should be kept very limited, exposing only what people need for their work. Trusted administrators should be blocked from changing their own critical access, and any action affecting superuser accounts, audit-only accounts, or users in another station should require a second approval or separate admin process. Disabling the last active administrator in a unit should not be allowed without oversight. For exceptional offline approvals, the system should capture the approver’s identity and role, the exact date and time of approval, the user who received the exception, the station or location, the case or incident reference, the specific offline action allowed, the reason for the exception, the expiry or review time if temporary, and whether the approval was made locally or escalated from a higher office.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

