[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-6-2-1",
    "slot_key": "Confidential data handling rules",
    "slot_value": "Restrict who can view or edit sensitive information; protect victim details, witness statements, and suspect data more tightly than general case information; apply stronger protection at complaint registration, evidence attachment, inter-unit sharing, and case closure or court handover. Access should be restricted when the material is sensitive, part of an open investigation, involves juvenile or victim privacy, or is legally sealed, and only approved users should be able to view it. In urgent operational situations, only authorized supervisors or designated officers may approve access to redacted or sensitive records; any bypass of normal controls must trigger escalation and mandatory after-the-fact review. A suggested investigative link may be shown normally only if the match is strong and recent, but it must still be blocked if the viewing officer lacks the right clearance or the linked data is privacy-restricted; if the match is weaker or the data is older, it should be shown with a caution label and not treated as confirmed evidence; if the match is low confidence or privacy rules do not allow the officer to see it at all, it must be hidden completely.",
    "operation": "refine"
  },
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-6-2-2",
    "slot_key": "Legal retention and disclosure obligations",
    "slot_value": "Keep an audit trail of every change and treat evidence as chain-of-custody material with clear time and officer records, especially when evidence is attached, shared between units, or handed over to court. Only authorized officers and records staff should be able to handle evidence, and every attachment or handoff should log the officer’s identity, date and time, unit, and what was transferred. If evidence moves between units, the system should keep a clear chain-of-custody trail so we can see who had it at each step and when. Updated records should keep the prior version in an audit history; duplicated records should carry a source reference; anything shared with another system should preserve the original retention status. Deletion or redaction should only happen when the retention period has expired or when there is a legal requirement to protect personal data, and even then a minimal audit record of the action should be kept. The receiving unit may create a restricted view or redacted copy for operational use, but the original record must remain intact in the source case file; the system must track what was redacted, who did it, when it was done, and why, while keeping the original retention rules tied to the master record. Further hiding by the receiving unit must not change the underlying evidence or audit history, only the visibility for that unit’s users. If audit logging cannot be fully captured in the moment, the system should require a manual incident note and late entry of the reason, approver, and affected records as soon as possible. Only authorized investigation or records staff should be able to add or replace documents, and the system must keep a clear audit trail of who did what and when. Viewing should be based on role and case access, with sensitive or evidentiary files restricted to people directly involved in the matter. Once a case has been shared, transferred, or moved forward, removing or replacing evidence-related files should be tightly controlled and usually require approval, because we need to preserve the integrity of the record. Suggested investigative links may be shown normally only if the match is strong and recent, but must still be blocked if clearance is insufficient or the linked data is privacy-restricted; weaker or older matches should be shown with a caution label and not treated as confirmed evidence; low-confidence links or links barred by privacy rules should be hidden completely.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

