    "slot_key": "Sensitive information access controls and audit trail",
    "slot_value": "When records are shared between police units during an active investigation, the receiving unit may create a restricted view or redacted copy for operational use, but the original record must remain intact in the source case file. The system should record what was redacted, who did it, when it was done, and why, while keeping the original retention rules tied to the master record. Further hiding by the receiving unit must only affect that unit's users and must not alter the underlying evidence or audit history. Exception handling should exist for urgent operational situations, with only authorized supervisors or designated officers able to approve access to redacted or sensitive records. Any bypass of normal controls should trigger escalation and a mandatory after-the-fact review. If audit logging cannot be fully captured in the moment, the system should require a manual incident note and late entry of the reason, approver, and affected records as soon as possible. It should also support audit logging so we can see who viewed or changed what. For restricted actions, formal approval from the station or district authority is needed, along with a role-based permission update and a clear audit trail explaining why the exception was granted. For manual escalation overrides, the audit trail should capture the user who made the override, the approver if there is one, the exact time, the case number, the status before and after, a short reason or justification, the unit involved, and any comments tied to the decision. The log should be protected so only authorized supervisory or audit users can view it; sensitive or sealed cases should be even more restricted; and it should not be deleted or hidden from oversight users.",
