    "slot_number": "slot-5-2-1",
    "slot_key": "Record update and audit trail expectations",
    "slot_value": "Keep the original record when a complaint moves between stations or higher authority, record where it was transferred, when, and to whom so the trail is not lost, and keep an audit trail of every change. Records staff should maintain the official complaint entry, ensure basic details are complete and standardized, correct obvious data issues, manage references, filing numbers, transfers, and the linkage between the complaint and the investigation record, and validate/preserve the proper version when official corrections to names or jurisdiction occur. In practice, the old paper or legacy record is first registered in the new system with a unique reference, the core details are verified (complainant name, date, location, current status), and records staff track what has been transferred while keeping the official master entry. During any short parallel period, updates must be entered into both systems or reconciled by records staff the same day so the versions do not drift apart; after cutover, one clearly designated source of truth must be used and any changes synchronized under that rule. If a case has been transferred or taken over by a higher office, the current controlling office should be the primary jurisdiction shown, but the original station or district name should still be kept in the history. Overrides to reopen a case, change ownership, or edit finalized notes should be allowed only in exceptional situations such as a clerical error, new evidence, a court return, or a supervisory review finding the case was closed too early, and must be approved by a supervisor or higher-ranking officer; in some cases both the originating and receiving unit may need to confirm. Any such override should be logged with the reason, approver, and timestamp so there is a clear audit trail. The initial foundation release should also include audit logging so it is possible to see who viewed or changed what. For manual escalation overrides, the audit trail should additionally capture the user who made the override, the approver if there is one, the exact time, the case number, the status before and after, a short reason or justification, the unit involved, and any comments tied to the decision. The log should be protected so only authorized supervisory or audit users can view it; for sensitive or sealed cases it should be even more restricted, but it must not be deleted or hidden from oversight users.",
